======================================================================
(A) tournament discriminant det(M): 0 for odd n, a SQUARE Pf^2 for even n
======================================================================
  n | #iso classes | discriminant values (det) | all squares? | Pfaffian values (even n)
  1 |           1 | [0]                        |         True | (0, odd n)
  2 |           1 | [1]                        |         True | [1]
  3 |           2 | [0]                        |         True | (0, odd n)
  4 |           4 | [1, 9]                     |         True | [1, 3]
  5 |          12 | [0]                        |         True | (0, odd n)
  6 |          56 | [1, 9, 25, 49, 81]         |         True | [1, 3, 5, 7, 9]
  -> odd n: det=0 (=0^2). even n: det=Pf^2, a perfect square -- ALWAYS a square,
     matching Aut(T)<=A_n (S643) & the falling-factorial disc model (S644).

======================================================================
(B) RECURSION: n->n-1 flips parity (0 <-> Pf^2); Pfaffian recurses n->n-2 (Mode B)
======================================================================
  the discriminant alternates: zero (odd) / nonzero-square (even). The square root
  (Pfaffian) of the even-n discriminant is built from (n-2)-Pfaffians by the cofactor
  expansion Pf(M)=sum_j (-1)^j M_1j Pf(M_hat{1,j}) -- the n->n-2 'both-legs' descent
  (CLAUDE.md Mode B). Mode A (n->n-1, add a vertex) is the parity flip.
  transitive T_2: det=1, Pf=1  (square 1^2)
  transitive T_3: det=0, Pf=0  (zero (odd))
  transitive T_4: det=1, Pf=1  (square 1^2)
  transitive T_5: det=0, Pf=0  (zero (odd))
  transitive T_6: det=1, Pf=1  (square 1^2)

======================================================================
(C) other invariants and their recursion as n grows
======================================================================
  n | #iso (A000568) | max|Aut| | #Ham-path values H (odd) | rank(M) values
  1 |              1 |        1 | [1]                      | [0]
  2 |              1 |        1 | [1]                      | [2]
  3 |              2 |        3 | [1, 3]                   | [2]
  4 |              4 |        3 | [1, 3, 5]                | [4]
  5 |             12 |        5 | [1, 3, 5, 9, 11, 13, 15] | [4]
  6 |             56 |        9 | [1, 3, 5, 9, 11, 13, 15, 17, 19, 23, 25, 27, 29, 31, 33, 37, 41, 43, 45] | [6]
  -> A000568 = 1,1,2,4,12,56 (the iso-class count, the master recursion).
  -> H (Rédei) always ODD; rank(M) = n (even, det!=0) or n-1 (odd, det=0): the
     skew rank drops by 1 exactly when n is odd = when the discriminant vanishes.
     So 'discriminant zero' <=> 'rank deficient by 1' <=> n odd -- the parity recursion.
